{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794474268,"nonce":"p9cE1FSJolPq2W0TcgwGqgNNy1g=","message":"Asked for on 2026-11-12 as \"close these gaps\", after an account of what stood between hxn and a useful library. This is the first three of them: data over time and in lent functions, enums whose variants hold values and maps, and failures Haxe can tell apart.\n\n**Data goes wherever a value goes.** `Type::Data(Data)` is data where it is carried: by a future, a stream or a signal, by what an async function gives, and to and from a lent function. A parameter or a result of data stays what it crosses as with the data noted beside it, as before.\n\n- Over time, the library's own future carries the library's own type, and the call that takes it writes it: `this.take::\u003cPoint\u003e()` then the bytes. So nothing in hxn-future changed, and a value is written on the Haxe thread that takes it, not the thread that settled it. A narrower number and `u64` are carried the same way, converted where taken; that was a refusal before.\n- The calls are named after the type, `future_take_list_point`, so two types that would share a name (`Vec\u003cPoint\u003e` and a record `ListPoint`) are refused when the library is read.\n- A signal of data needs the data `Clone`, which is hxn-future's bound and is met in the adapter, not in the library's crate: the error names generated code.\n- In a lent function the adapter's closure writes each argument before the call and reads the result after. It borrows through `std::borrow::Borrow` so that `Fn(Point)` and `Fn(\u0026Point)`, `Fn(Vec\u003cT\u003e)` and `Fn(\u0026[T])` are one piece of generated code; a plain `\u0026a0` with a type written beside it is what clippy calls a needless borrow when the argument is already a reference. `Fn(\u0026Vec\u003cT\u003e)` is not taken.\n- A lent function gives bytes back now, `Vec\u003cu8\u003e`, and data as bytes. On hxcpp the wrapper returns the `BytesData` and the trampoline copies it into a buffer with its own `free`, as text was. On HashLink the first version returned `hl.Bytes` with the length in the first four bytes: stock HashLink boxes a closure's `hl.Bytes` result in a dynamic and Ash hands back the bare pointer, so reading it one way crashed on the other (a segfault in Ash's hybrid mode, in the first run). The wrapper returns a `haxe.io.Bytes` instead, an object on both, and `hxn_hl::buffer` reads its two fields; the primitive's signature writes it `OiB_`, so a Haxe whose `haxe.io.Bytes` is laid out otherwise fails when the library loads and not when it runs.\n\n**Enums whose variants hold values, boxes and maps.** `Data::Tagged`, `Data::Boxed`, `Data::Map`.\n\n- A tagged enum is a Haxe `enum` of the same cases; a field Rust leaves unnamed is `value`, or `value0`, `value1`. On the wire it is which case, counted from 0, then the fields. The adapter binds fields as `f0`, `f1`: a field called `out` or `input` would otherwise shadow the function's own.\n- A Haxe enum has no functions, so `\u003cName\u003eWire` beside it writes and reads. One class a library for every enum was weighed: it would have meant passing the library's name through every place data is written. `\u003cName\u003eWire` is checked against the library's other names.\n- `Box\u003cT\u003e` is its `T` everywhere but in Rust, which is what lets data hold its own kind. The reader of a record or a tagged enum counts how deep it is, `wire::Reader::DEPTH` (128), and fails with the new `Status::DEPTH` past it. The limit is on reading only: a library can write deeper than it would read.\n- An Emscripten build's stack is too small to write a value 200 deep in a debug build (\"memory access out of bounds\" from node), so the fixture's check that a deep value is refused is not made there. The limit of 128 has not been shown to fit that stack with frames larger than the fixture's.\n- A map is `HashMap` or `BTreeMap`, keyed by `String`, `i32`, or an 8 or 16 bit integer: what a Haxe `Map` is keyed by without an abstract in the way. Reads are a statement each on the Haxe side, so the key is read before the value whatever the target does with argument order.\n- WebIDL has no sum type, and a union of dictionaries is not valid, so a tagged enum is a dictionary with a `tag` and a member a case. A map is a `record\u003cUSVString, V\u003e` whatever its key.\n\n**A failure that is data.** `Method::error` is set where the error of the `Result` is the library's own `#[hxn::data]`. Haxe is thrown `\u003cName\u003eException`, a `haxe.Exception` with the error's `Display` as its message and the value as `error`.\n\n- Why an exception class and not the value thrown as it is: `hxn.Future.then` hands `onError` a `haxe.Exception`, and `await` keeps one; a thrown enum would arrive there as a `ValueException` with its message lost; and an enum of names is an `Int` to a `catch`.\n- How the value crosses: `hxn_abi::Failure` gained `value`, bytes. `guard` and `hxn_hl::prim` keep it per thread (`hxn_abi::thrown`) and throw the message as ever. The generated method catches `haxe.Exception`, asks the library for the kept bytes (`\u003clibrary\u003e_thrown_take`), and throws the typed exception, or the same one if there were none. The bytes start with which of the library's errors it is.\n- Weighed: framing the value into the error buffer under a new status. hxcpp's glue and HashLink's `prim` would each then have to throw something that carries bytes, which means native code making a Haxe object or a second kept slot on that side; keeping it in Rust is one place for both. And a thread-local alone, without the field on `Failure`: a future is settled on one thread and taken on another.\n- Every failure replaces what is kept, so a stale value cannot be mistaken for the next failure's.\n- A future's take is wrapped in every library that has such an error, since one take serves every function that returns a future of that type.\n- The `?` in such a method converts a `Failure` to itself, which clippy reports, so the method allows it as one that lends functions does.\n\nFixture: `tests/counter/core/src/events.rs` and `events()` in `Generated.hx`. Example: `examples/pantry` gained `take`, which fails with `PantryError`.\n\nNot built:\n- A typed failure of a stream. A stream's failure is a message.\n- An object or an `Option` of one given back by a lent function; `i64` to or from one.\n- A default for a field, a map keyed by an enum, a set, a tuple.\n- Reading WebIDL.\n\nRun on 2026-11-12: `tests/counter/run.sh gen-hl` on Ash (hybrid, jit, interp, three times each) and stock HashLink on the NUC (three times), `gen-cpp`, `gen-cppia`, `gen-wasm`, `gen-emcc`; `tests/examples/run.sh` and `tests/cli/run.sh` on Ash and hxcpp; `cargo miri test -p hxn-abi` (34 tests) and `-p hxn-future`, where 30 pass and the four that fail are the four CI skips under Miri for counting on time. Also `tests/mobile/run.sh` on an iOS simulator and an Android emulator, which is recorded on the command's issue. Not run: Windows, and hxcpp on Linux.\n\nCommitted as a902102, not pushed.","files":null}]}